WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:03.120
TIKTOK INCOME:
Pay on TikTok is horrendous. Now,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:03.120 --> 00:00:09.000
before you go to comments section and leave angry&nbsp;
remarks, let me explain. TikTok only pays about&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:09.000 --> 00:00:15.360
$0.02 to $0.04 per 1,000 views, so even if you get&nbsp;
a ridiculous amount of views, you’re not making&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:15.360 --> 00:00:21.660
much. Take Khaby Lame for example. He’s the most&nbsp;
followed TikToker on the planet with a whopping&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:21.660 --> 00:00:28.800
159 million followers and nearly 18 billion&nbsp;
views. But, in terms of ad revenue from TikTok,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:28.800 --> 00:00:36.360
those 18 billion views only translates to&nbsp;
$540,000. That’s not a bad amount by any means,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:36.360 --> 00:00:42.900
but keep in mind, we’re talking about the most&nbsp;
followed TikToker in the world. For perspective,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:42.900 --> 00:00:48.960
if you got that same number of views on YouTube,&nbsp;
your total earnings would be closer to $54 million&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:48.960 --> 00:00:56.040
assuming a $3 RPM. Now of course, these TikTokers&nbsp;
don’t just rely on TikTok adsense. They also do&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:56.040 --> 00:01:00.060
brand deals and sponsorships and affiliate&nbsp;
marketing and all that stuff which pulls&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:00.060 --> 00:01:05.760
in a whole lot of money. But, the disparity&nbsp;
between TikTok adsense and YouTube adsense is&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:05.760 --> 00:01:12.660
still staggering. I mean, we’re talking about&nbsp;
a 100X difference. And it’s not just creators&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:12.660 --> 00:01:18.360
who are feeling the pain. TikTok is also very&nbsp;
much feeling the pain having lost $7 billion&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:18.360 --> 00:01:24.120
last year and it’s only getting worse. Vine&nbsp;
was facing this same challenge 10 years ago&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:24.120 --> 00:01:29.640
and this is the main reasons that they eventually&nbsp;
shut down. All of their most popular Viners would&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:29.640 --> 00:01:34.800
translate their audiences over to YouTube.&nbsp;
This includes Drew Gooden, David Dobrik,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:34.800 --> 00:01:41.220
Danny Gonzales, Cody Ko, and of course Logan&nbsp;
Paul and Jake Paul.. We’re starting to see&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:41.220 --> 00:01:46.860
the exact same thing happen with TikTokers as&nbsp;
well. Notable examples are Charli D’Amelio,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:46.860 --> 00:01:51.339
Bella Poarch, Addison Rae, and KallMeKris.&nbsp;
Fun fact, some of these creators have already&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:51.339 --> 00:01:54.300
earned substantially more on YouTube than TikTok.&nbsp;
KallMeKris for example has earned around $350,000&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:54.300 --> 00:02:00.660
on TikTok but she’s already earned millions on&nbsp;
YouTube. This is not a great sign for TikTok&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:01.560 --> 00:02:07.440
as this is hard evidence that people are simply&nbsp;
using TikTok as a springboard for YouTube or to&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:07.440 --> 00:02:11.820
jumpstart their music career while the platform&nbsp;
is burning billions trying to stay afloat. So,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:11.820 --> 00:02:16.740
here’s TikTok’s monetization crisis and&nbsp;
the uncertain future of the platform.

00:02:16.740 --> 00:02:19.020
HESITANT ADVERTISERS:&nbsp;

00:02:19.020 --> 00:02:29.160
By far the largest shortcoming of TikTok&nbsp;
is advertisers. Advertisers simply do not&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:29.160 --> 00:02:34.260
want to advertise on TikTok. It’s easy to&nbsp;
blame the medium for this reluctance. Most&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:34.260 --> 00:02:39.720
advertisers simply aren’t used to making short&nbsp;
form ads in portrait mode. And this is true to&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:39.720 --> 00:02:45.540
a certain degree but it’s not the main reason.&nbsp;
I mean companies like Apple and Coca Cola have&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:45.540 --> 00:02:50.820
marketing budgets in the hundreds of millions&nbsp;
if not billions. And they have hundreds if not&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:50.820 --> 00:02:56.340
thousands of employees who’s full time job is&nbsp;
to simply figure this out. So, the reason that&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:56.340 --> 00:03:01.740
companies are reluctant to advertise on TikTok is&nbsp;
not because they don’t know how or can’t figure it&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:01.740 --> 00:03:08.940
out, it’s because they don’t want to. Why you ask?&nbsp;
Well, for one, it’s not a very lucrative audience.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:08.940 --> 00:03:15.300
41% of TikTok users are between the ages of 16&nbsp;
and 24 which means that most of them don’t have&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:15.300 --> 00:03:21.000
any money. For perspective, this is how YouTube’s&nbsp;
audience demographic looks like. As you can see,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:21.000 --> 00:03:27.360
it’s a lot more evenly dispersed. This in itself&nbsp;
is a big turn off for advertisers but the issue&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:27.360 --> 00:03:33.180
goes much deeper than just demographics. Likely&nbsp;
the biggest concern for most advertisers is brand&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:33.180 --> 00:03:40.080
optics. It’s no secret that the content on TikTok&nbsp;
is how should I say it “edgy”. You’ve got thirst&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:40.080 --> 00:03:45.180
traps, a bunch of cursing, and all this other&nbsp;
stuff that YouTube tells you not to include. This&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:45.180 --> 00:03:50.400
is actually what has made TikTok so popular in&nbsp;
the first place but there’s a reason that YouTube&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:50.400 --> 00:03:56.280
doesn’t lean into this content. Advertisers&nbsp;
simply do not want to be associated with&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:56.280 --> 00:04:01.620
this type of content. I mean, if you were Apple,&nbsp;
would you really want your ad to play after this?&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:01.620 --> 00:04:08.100
I’m gonna guess no. And this makes a far larger&nbsp;
difference than you might think. At this point,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:08.100 --> 00:04:13.860
I think we can agree that TikTok is very much&nbsp;
a worldwide platform. In 2021, it was literally&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:13.860 --> 00:04:19.560
the world’s most popular domain for crying out&nbsp;
loud. So, clearly, it’s extremely popular across&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:19.560 --> 00:04:26.520
the globe but guess what? 80% of TikTok’s revenue&nbsp;
still comes from China. And if you didn’t know,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:26.520 --> 00:04:32.700
the TikTok that we use is actually banned in&nbsp;
China. You see, the CCP decided that mainstream&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:32.700 --> 00:04:38.340
TikTok is simply too vulgar and unproductive&nbsp;
for the youth. So, instead, they have a healthy&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:38.340 --> 00:04:43.920
version of TikTok called Douyin. Fundamentally&nbsp;
speaking, it’s the same app. The difference is&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:43.920 --> 00:04:48.960
the type of content that is posted and promoted on&nbsp;
the platform. Douyin primarily promotes science,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:48.960 --> 00:04:54.540
educational, and historical content instead&nbsp;
of lip syncs and dancing. Furthermore, younger&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:54.540 --> 00:04:59.940
generations are limited to just 40 minutes of&nbsp;
Douyin per day, so no hours of mindless scrolling.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:00.540 --> 00:05:06.360
Douyin is extremely popular in China but it&nbsp;
doesn’t quite stack up to TikTok. Douyin boasts&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:06.360 --> 00:05:12.600
730 million monhtly active users while TikTok&nbsp;
boasts 1.05 billion monthly active users. So,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:12.600 --> 00:05:19.380
TikTok is not only more popular but people tend to&nbsp;
spend far more time on TikTok. But despite this,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:19.380 --> 00:05:25.320
China is able account for 80% of TikTok’s revenue&nbsp;
because advertisers are actually interested&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:25.320 --> 00:05:31.260
in appearing next to Douyin videos. So, until&nbsp;
TikTok is able to address advertiser hesitation,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:31.260 --> 00:05:37.140
TikTok will continue to face major monetization&nbsp;
issues leading to more creators migrating away.

00:05:37.140 --> 00:05:41.340
SHORT FORM HELL:&nbsp;

00:05:41.340 --> 00:05:49.620
Advertisers no doubt play a massive role&nbsp;
in how much is paid out to creators,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:49.620 --> 00:05:55.800
but they’re not the only issue plaguing TikTok.&nbsp;
You see, short form is simply fundamentally&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:55.800 --> 00:06:00.840
harder to monetize because views are worth&nbsp;
substantially less. On TikTok, for example,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:00.840 --> 00:06:06.600
the average video is only 36 to 42 seconds&nbsp;
depending on the size of the account. Even&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:06.600 --> 00:06:12.240
if we assume that these videos have 75% audience&nbsp;
retention on average, each view only translates&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:12.240 --> 00:06:18.360
to about 30 seconds of watch time. Now, compare&nbsp;
that to what we see on YouTube. Using myself as&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:18.360 --> 00:06:23.520
an example, my average video gets just under 7&nbsp;
minutes of average watch time, which by the way&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:23.520 --> 00:06:30.300
thank you. This means that the average view on my&nbsp;
channel is worth about 14 views on TikTok. Why do&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:30.300 --> 00:06:35.940
I bring this up? Well, monetization has more to&nbsp;
do with watch time than views. I tend to put an&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:35.940 --> 00:06:41.580
ad break once every 3 minutes which means that&nbsp;
the average view translates 3 ad impressions.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:41.580 --> 00:06:47.520
For simplicity, let’s say that TikTok shows an ad&nbsp;
once every 3 minutes as well. Since each video is&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:47.520 --> 00:06:51.900
only watched for 30 seconds at best, a viewer&nbsp;
would have to scroll through 6 TikToks before&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:51.900 --> 00:06:58.200
reaching an ad and that’s the best case scenario.&nbsp;
More likely, they’ll have to scroll through 10 or&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:58.200 --> 00:07:04.560
15 TikToks before seeing an ad but we’ll call it 6&nbsp;
to give them the benefit of the doubt. This means&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:04.560 --> 00:07:11.040
that each view on TikTok only translates to 1/6th&nbsp;
of an ad impression. Putting this next to YouTube,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:11.040 --> 00:07:17.580
we’ll see that each YouTube view translates to&nbsp;
18 times more ad impressions. So, if we want to&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:17.580 --> 00:07:22.380
really create an accurate comparison between how&nbsp;
much advertisers pay on YouTube vs how much they&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:22.380 --> 00:07:28.020
pay on TikTok, we can’t compare views to revenue,&nbsp;
we have to compare ad impressions to revenue.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:28.020 --> 00:07:34.500
YouTube generally pays about $3 to $5 per thousand&nbsp;
views in most niches. And as we previously touched&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:34.500 --> 00:07:40.800
on, TikTok pays 2 to 4 cents per thousand views.&nbsp;
But, if we normalize based on ad impressions,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:40.800 --> 00:07:46.380
we’ll see that TikTok is actually paying 36 to 72&nbsp;
cents for the same number of ad impressions that&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:46.380 --> 00:07:53.040
you would get from 1000 YouTube views. Still about&nbsp;
7-8 times worse than YouTube but also 18 times&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:53.040 --> 00:07:58.140
better than what the upfront numbers suggested.&nbsp;
The only way to work around this obstacle is&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:58.140 --> 00:08:03.360
move towards longer form content which is exactly&nbsp;
what TikTok and its creators have been doing. What&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:03.360 --> 00:08:09.600
started at a maximum video length of 7 seconds has&nbsp;
grown all the way to 10 minutes. TikTok has also&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:09.600 --> 00:08:14.760
been testing with landscape mode, but increasing&nbsp;
the average view duration isn’t as easy as just&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:14.760 --> 00:08:19.800
getting creators to post longer videos. The&nbsp;
algorithm highly values audience retention,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:19.800 --> 00:08:24.780
so if TikTokers want their long form videos to&nbsp;
perform well, they have to increase video length&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:24.780 --> 00:08:31.200
without sacrificing audience retention. Also, we&nbsp;
see all those articles about our ever decreasing&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:31.200 --> 00:08:38.100
attention spans. And if that’s true anywhere, it’s&nbsp;
true on TikTok. So, TikTok is very much fighting&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:38.100 --> 00:08:43.140
an uphill battle when it comes to monetization&nbsp;
due to the fundamental nature of their content.

00:08:43.140 --> 00:08:51.960
GIVING UP:
Hearing all this, you might be saying&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:51.960 --> 00:08:57.600
that this isn’t just an issue on TikTok. Instagram&nbsp;
and YouTube have also been promoting short form&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:57.600 --> 00:09:03.840
content. So, they should be facing the same issues&nbsp;
right? Well, you’re right, YouTube and Instagram&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:03.840 --> 00:09:09.360
are indeed facing the same issues. With YouTube&nbsp;
shorts, for example, you can expect to earn 5&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:09.360 --> 00:09:15.900
cents per thousand views. But, here’s the thing,&nbsp;
YouTube and Instagram are not tied down by short&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:15.900 --> 00:09:21.840
form content. Short form content is not their&nbsp;
golden goose. It’s simply an expansion effort.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:21.840 --> 00:09:28.140
And they’re already starting to decide that this&nbsp;
expansion effort isn’t worth it. You see, platform&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:28.140 --> 00:09:33.480
monetization is a two sided coin. If creators&nbsp;
aren’t earning much money from ads, neither is&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:33.480 --> 00:09:38.340
the platform. At least, the creators can go out&nbsp;
and get brand deals and sponsors and affiliates&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:38.340 --> 00:09:44.820
and all that. For these platforms, their only&nbsp;
revenue is ad revenue. So, if that’s garbage, the&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:44.820 --> 00:09:50.400
entire revenue from the whole effort is garbage,&nbsp;
and that’s what seems to be playing out. In fact,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:50.400 --> 00:09:56.640
according to Mark Zuckerberg, revenue from Reels&nbsp;
is so garbage that Meta is losing $500 million per&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:56.640 --> 00:10:02.100
quarter because of users watching Reels instead&nbsp;
of scrolling through pictures. Yeah, I don’t&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:02.100 --> 00:10:07.320
think you’d be surprised to hear that Meta has&nbsp;
stopped offering Reels bonuses to creators. So,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:07.320 --> 00:10:12.720
while Instagram and YouTube are very much in the&nbsp;
same position as TikTok with shorts, they’re also&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:12.720 --> 00:10:17.940
not. They have the luxury of pulling back and&nbsp;
focusing on what they were already doing. The&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:17.940 --> 00:10:24.780
same cannot be said about TikTok. But then what&nbsp;
is the solution? Well, unfortunately for TikTok,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:24.780 --> 00:10:30.660
there is no clear solution to their monetization&nbsp;
problem. Again, this is literally why Vine shut&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:30.660 --> 00:10:37.080
down. What TikTok really needs is time. They need&nbsp;
time for brands to become more open with the idea&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:37.080 --> 00:10:42.120
of advertising on TikTok. In the meantime, their&nbsp;
best bet is to tinker with the algorithm to&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:42.120 --> 00:10:47.820
push longer form content that’s more advertiser&nbsp;
friendly. But, this is also a pretty big gamble.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:47.820 --> 00:10:54.120
This can lead to a sort ad apocalypse like we saw&nbsp;
with YouTube back in 2017. More edgy creators got&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:54.120 --> 00:10:59.400
pushed off the platform as family friendly content&nbsp;
got pushed to the top. But, the thing to note is&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:59.400 --> 00:11:04.620
that YouTube always had a good mix edgy and family&nbsp;
friendly content, so the shift towards family&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:11:04.620 --> 00:11:10.860
friendly content was more natural. With TikTok on&nbsp;
the other hand, we’re primarily dealing with edgy&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:11:10.860 --> 00:11:16.980
content so the shift is a lot more jarring, and&nbsp;
it’s not clear how many viewers TikTok would push&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:11:16.980 --> 00:11:22.920
off the platform by making such a change. But,&nbsp;
with their peers already dropping out and pulling&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:11:22.920 --> 00:11:27.900
back, this might be the hail mary that TikTok&nbsp;
needs to make to stay relevant over the long term.

00:11:27.900 --> 00:11:34.440
THE STATE OF TIKTOK:&nbsp;

00:11:34.440 --> 00:11:40.860
As you can see, TikTok is very much facing a&nbsp;
monetization crisis and there is no easy solution,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:11:40.860 --> 00:11:46.320
but there is a silver lining for TikTok. Unlike&nbsp;
Vine which was backed by Twitter which was in&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:11:46.320 --> 00:11:51.420
itself not a profitable company, TikTok is&nbsp;
backed by Bytedance which has been posting&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:11:51.420 --> 00:11:58.200
record profits. In fact, they made $25 billion&nbsp;
last year. So, losses from TikTok are definitely&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:11:58.200 --> 00:12:04.200
something that they can sustain even over the long&nbsp;
term. Also, even if Bytedance can’t pull it off on&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:12:04.200 --> 00:12:09.480
their own, they’ve got the backing of the CCP.&nbsp;
You can bet that as long as TikTok is popular,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:12:09.480 --> 00:12:14.700
the CCP won’t let them go under as&nbsp;
TikTok is literally a goldmine for them.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:12:14.700 --> 00:12:20.520
So, from a financial perspective, they very much&nbsp;
have time on their side. But they may not be able&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:12:20.520 --> 00:12:25.500
to say the same thing about their creators or&nbsp;
viewers. Their creators are naturally pivoting&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:12:25.500 --> 00:12:34.980
towards YouTube to build stronger businesses&nbsp;
with more revenue. And as for viewers, TikTok&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:12:34.980 --> 00:12:39.720
faces the risk of their viewers growing up, kind&nbsp;
of like what SnapChat is dealing with right now.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:12:39.720 --> 00:12:45.060
So while TikTok may not be on the clock for&nbsp;
financial reasons, they’re very much on the clock&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:12:45.060 --> 00:12:50.880
due to trends amongst their viewers and creators.&nbsp;
Will they be able to beat the clock? Only time&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:12:50.880 --> 00:12:56.100
will tell. While all of this madness has been&nbsp;
going on with short form content, podcasts have&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:12:56.100 --> 00:13:01.500
actually been doing better than ever on YouTube.&nbsp;
If you wanna learn why, check out this video.

